We will now work on making our system bootable. The boot chain for our setup
looks roughly like this:
     1. the boot loader systemd-boot is executed by the UEFI firmware
     2. the boot loader executes a kernel file, which packages the different
         parts required for booting Linux into a a single executable
     3. it passes arguments, such as the location of the root partition to the
         kernel through the command line; the command line is read either from a
         config file or may be entered directly in the boot loader
     4. the kernel loads an initial RAM file system (initramfs), a basic
         environment that includes everything needed to bootstrap the system
     5. the initramfs contains a systemd executable, which will look at an
         embedded crypttab file to unlock the volumes described within
     6. after successfully unlocking the device holding the root partition,
         systemd will attempt to mount it using the information given to the
         kernel through the command line
     7. once early boot has finished, control will be handed over from the
         initramfs to the system

Understanding the general boot chain is useful, as it helps when troubleshoot
any errors that may occur. While all the components described above will be
covered by this script, we will make use of unified kernel images (UKI) that
package them into a single UEFI executable.
